// name A (necessarily) brief history of the remainder of mathematics.
// id math-remainder
// +# blog math-remainder
// +# timeline 2026/09/20
// +tags math

In a few years, an AI will produce a series of papers or a monograph
entitled something like

>[...Co-prismatic Eisenstein Theory I: Deconstruction of Semi-euleroid Motifs]
/ += . "^.*$" -> https://ems.press/journals/prims/articles/201525

A typical mathematician will be able to follow the text as far as page
five. A few people on the planet will reach page ten. On page thirty,
out of two hundred, there will be an unnumbered proposition: "From the
last three lemmata, the Riemann hypothesis obviously follows."
/ += . "Riemann hypothesis" -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_hypothesis

The work will quickly be verified by other AIs. It will be formalised
and verified in Lean or, rather, in a much more powerful proof
assistant that an AI will have created some time before and in which
the entirety of human-produced mathematics will have been verified.
/ += . "Lean" -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lean_(proof_assistant)

You will be able to ask an AI to explain the results at a level you
can understand, and it will gladly do so, but reading the diluted
explanations necessary to reach page twenty will take decades. Now you
know what angels feel before the face of god: a presence of an
infinitely superior, seemingly benevolent, if aloof, intelligence.

It will not stop there, of course. More papers will be produced, using
the final conclusions of the original work --inaccessible to you-- as
starting points. And then more.

And given that, after a century of effort involving some of the world’s
leading mathematicians, an AI has produced a comically simple
counter-example to the Jacobian conjecture---one that an eighth-grader
can verify, it is no longer seem entirely impossible that it might,
after all, find an elementary proof of Fermat’s Last Theorem that fits
within the default LaTeX margins.
/ += . "Jacobian conjecture" -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacobian_conjecture
